---
layout: default
---
{% comment %}
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  LOCAL OVERRIDE of the `cv` layout owned by al_folio_core (copied from v1.0.15).

  Why this file exists: the `cv` layout never renders `{{ content }}`, so
  markdown in _pages/cv.md is silently discarded — there is no way to put a
  banner on that page from the page itself. `{% al_folio_cv_render %}` then
  emits the entire article as one opaque block (header, <h1>, PDF link and all),
  so the banner cannot be slotted between the title and the CV body without
  reimplementing the whole render. Above the header is the only insertion point,
  which is where a banner wants to be anyway.

  Set the image in the page's front matter:

      banner: assets/img/brain_slices.jpg

  Everything else is verbatim from the gem. ON UPGRADING al_folio_core: diff
  this against the gem's own _layouts/cv.liquid and re-apply the banner block,
  or delete this file if the gem grows a banner hook of its own. Find the gem's
  copy with:  gem contents al_folio_core | grep layouts/cv
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
{% endcomment %}
{% assign cv_feature_enabled = site.al_folio.features.cv.enabled | default: true %}

{% if site.plugins contains 'al_folio_cv' and cv_feature_enabled %}
  {% if page.banner %}
    {% comment %} Decorative only: alt="" keeps screen readers from announcing
       it. figure.liquid omits the attribute entirely when alt is not passed,
       which is worse — some readers fall back to reading the filename. {% endcomment %}
    <div class="cv-banner">
      {% include figure.liquid
         path=page.banner
         alt=""
         loading="eager"
         sizes="95vw"
         class="img-fluid rounded" %}
    </div>
  {% endif %}
  {% al_folio_cv_render %}
{% else %}
  <div class="post">
    <header class="post-header">
      <h1 class="post-title">{{ page.title }}</h1>
      <p class="post-description">CV rendering is unavailable.</p>
    </header>
    <article>
      <p>Enable the <code>al_folio_cv</code> plugin and set <code>al_folio.features.cv.enabled: true</code> to render this page.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
{% endif %}
